{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>Who am I, that I should go to Pharaoh, and that I should bring the children of Israel out.</b> It appears to me that Moses said: from two sides I am unfit for this mission — both from the side of the lowliness of the emissary, and from the side of the greatness of Israel's merit. For Moses was the humblest of all men, and in his own eyes he was a lowly person; therefore he said, 'Who am I, that I should go to Pharaoh' — is it fitting that a lowly person such as I should speak before the king? And from the side of Israel's merit he said, 'and that I should bring the children of Israel out' — for a nation so exalted and lofty as this, the children of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, a great and noble people — how shall they follow after one lowly and despised as I am? To both of these the Holy One, blessed be He, answered him: 'For I will be with you' — and it is not you who will speak to Pharaoh, but I will be the one speaking, and your mouth is as My mouth; also, Israel will not follow after you but after HaShem they will follow, since indeed I will be with you. And if you would say: from the very outset this is a difficulty — why should the Holy One, blessed be He, be with you specifically, and why should He not be with some great and honored person of distinction, and why should He choose to join Himself to lowly people specifically to appoint him as emissary? — to this He gave him a sign, that such is the attribute of the Holy One, blessed be He: He chooses most to join Himself to the lowly, as it says, ״יִשְׁכּוֹן אֶת דַּכָּא וּשְׁפַל רוּחַ״ (Isaiah 57:15). And this is what He said: 'And this shall be the sign for you, that I have sent you' — for if they say to you, why did HaShem choose you to be an emissary more than the rest? you shall say to them that such is the attribute of the Holy One, blessed be He, for behold, 'when you bring the people out of Egypt, you shall serve Elohim on this mountain' — Mount Sinai, the lowest of the mountains. Even though there are mountains higher than it, nevertheless the Holy One, blessed be He, chose only this Sinai; and from this you shall take proof that the Holy One, blessed be He, chooses the lowly most of all — and this too is a miracle attesting to your mission." "<b>But one must consider:</b> What is written elsewhere (Exodus 20:2), ״אָנֹכִי ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ אֲשֶׁר הוֹצֵאתִיךָ מֵאֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם״ — but here He did not mention 'land,' and said only, ״וְהוֹצֵא אֶת עַמִּי בְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל מִמִּצְרָיִם״. Also the word ״עַמִּי״ ('my people') seems superfluous. Therefore it appears to me that this bringing out does not speak of the departure from the land, but rather that they should go out from the deeds of the Egyptians. For Israel became defiled by the idolatry of Egypt, as is found in Ezekiel (20:5), ״וָאִוָּדַע לָהֶם בְּאֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם״ etc., and from the entire passage there it is implied that the Holy One, blessed be He, sent to them several times to warn Israel not to defile themselves with the idolatry of Egypt, but they refused to listen. Therefore also on this occasion the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses, ״וְהוֹצֵא אֶת עַמִּי בְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל מִמִּצְרָיִם״ — to bring them out from the deeds of Egypt, that they not defile themselves any further with their idolatry. Even though they had already become defiled by them, nevertheless they are still 'My people' — for even though Israel sinned, they are still Israel (Sanhedrin 44a). This is the meaning of 'My people, the children of Israel.' And this matter was very difficult in Moses' eyes, for he knew that Israel had not heeded the emissaries who had already gone to them to bring them out from the idolatry of Egypt. Therefore he said, 'and that I should bring the children of Israel out of Egypt — for what reason would they come to abandon their habitual practice?' And to this the Holy One, blessed be He, answered him, 'when you bring the people out of Egypt, you shall serve HaShem on this mountain.' Through their receiving the Torah, their spiritual contamination would cease, as our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Shabbat 146a): 'Israel who stood at Mount Sinai — their contamination ceased,' for the standing at Mount Sinai was a great miracle for Israel attesting to His divinity, blessed be He, and through this they departed completely from the idolatry of Egypt.'"], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:18:19.891731341Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}